From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] ui/cocoa: start animation, then Omarchy's logo instead of "Display output is not active."

What: when the window opens, OMACVM turns into Omarchy's logo (O, M and A
stay, the cells of C, V and M fly and land as R, C, H and Y; 2.4 s), Omarchy
green on Omarchy's background, then the logo holds until the guest shows its
first picture and fades into it (the firmware's: the same logo, same size,
same place). Reduce motion or OMACVM_SPLASH_ANIMATION=0: the logo without the
morph. If the guest's picture comes before the animation ends, the rest plays
within 0.35 s, then the fade. Only the main window animates.
After that, until the guest shows a picture on an output, and when it switches
the output off again (a reboot), the window shows Omarchy's logo, green on
black, where the firmware draws it, instead of QEMU's placeholder text
("Display output is not active.", "Guest has not initialized the display
(yet)."). The windows of the other displays show it on Omarchy's background.
If the guest has shown nothing of its own 90 s of running time after it
started (OMACVM_SPLASH_HINT_SECONDS), a line under the logo says Omarchy is
taking long and where its logs are (OMACVM_LOGS, set by the launcher), and
qemu.log gets a warning. OMACVM_BOOT_SPLASH=0 brings QEMU's text back and
turns the animation off. The main window's layer now draws again when the
window changes size.

Why: every start showed QEMU's text for a few seconds (before the firmware,
and from GRUB until Linux draws), and every reboot for longer. It looked like
an error but is just the guest getting ready. The animation is the one the
user picked from three previews (a-pixel-morph).

How: the animation is a Core Animation layer over the window's GL layer (a
shape layer for the cells, sharp at any size and scale; a 640 x 360 image
under it for the glow), driven by a CADisplayLink on the main thread; the
guest is never held up. Its core (no Cocoa, no QEMU) is a port of the
preview's generator: the flying cells' table is made by
boot-logo/make-splash-morph.py, the glow is the preview's blur (Pillow's
resampling weights). The still logo is drawn in the window's GL layers: an
81 x 19 texture with sharp cells, placed where the firmware's stretched frame
has its logo (1920 x 1080 on a cold start, after a reboot the size of the
guest's last picture, as virtio-gpu then reports), so neither the hand-over to
the firmware nor the animation's last frame moves it. The logo is the
firmware's (boot-logo/make-logo-bmp.py logo.svg --rows, from Omarchy's
logo.svg, MIT), 15 pixels a cell in the firmware's frame.

Upstream: OmacVM only (branding), not for QEMU.

Test: Tests/display/test-boot-splash-cells.py (the cells make exactly the
firmware's Logo.bmp of edk2-logo-omarchy.patch, at SPLASH_CELL);
make-splash-morph.py --check (the table is the generator's);
Tests/display/test-boot-splash-morph.c (the core starts as OMACVM and ends as
the logo, still, on the pixels GL's nearest sampling gives at several window
sizes; no jumps; the glow comes and goes). The build runs all three;
Tests/display/check-boot-splash.sh runs them from this patch alone (CI).
Frames of the core against the preview's: start and end identical, mid-flight
under 0.1 % of pixels differ by more than 8 of 255. The start, hand-over,
Reduce motion and a reboot were checked in the app (tracks/boot-splash in the
work notes).

